./data/living_catalog_updates/update_2026-08-09/15_atlas/data/atlas.json:16729:        "description": "SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nWorkflow/model architecture diagram. It shows DNA sequence, protein sequence, and natural language inputs passed through a unified BPE tokenization/encoder, then pre-training of a GPT-2 model labeled `GPT2-gene-eng`. The model is fine-tuned using PAWS English datasets and a sequence similarity classification dataset, producing `GPT2-gene-eng-ft`. Further fine-tuning branches use English summary datasets and English paragraph segmentation datasets, yielding models labeled `gene_eng_gpt2_para_seg` and `gene_eng_gpt2_summary`.\n\nNo axes or quantitative plots are present. The diagram depicts language capability transfer from English to DNA, with one branch marked “Verifiable” and a later genome-book application marked as “Unverified?” / “Indirect Verification?”. The visible main idea is a proposed pipeline for transferring natural-language modeling capabilities to genomic sequence tasks through unified tokenization and GPT-2 pretraining/fine-tuning.",
./data/living_catalog_updates/update_2026-08-09/15_atlas/data/atlas.json:16887:          "evidence_quote": "We primarily used the OpenWebText dataset, combined with Wikipedia data, as the natural language training dataset, focusing on English text.",
./data/living_catalog_updates/update_2026-08-09/15_atlas/data/atlas.json:16973:        "description": "SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nWorkflow/model architecture diagram. It shows DNA sequence, protein sequence, and natural language inputs passed through a unified BPE tokenization/encoder, then pre-training of a GPT-2 model labeled `GPT2-gene-eng`. The model is fine-tuned using PAWS English datasets and a sequence similarity classification dataset, producing `GPT2-gene-eng-ft`. Further fine-tuning branches use English summary datasets and English paragraph segmentation datasets, yielding models labeled `gene_eng_gpt2_para_seg` and `gene_eng_gpt2_summary`.\n\nNo axes or quantitative plots are present. The diagram depicts language capability transfer from English to DNA, with one branch marked “Verifiable” and a later genome-book application marked as “Unverified?” / “Indirect Verification?”. The visible main idea is a proposed pipeline for transferring natural-language modeling capabilities to genomic sequence tasks through unified tokenization and GPT-2 pretraining/fine-tuning.",
./data/living_catalog_updates/update_2026-08-09/15_atlas/data/atlas.json:18692:      "no_figure_rationale": "The current crop from Extended Data Fig. 1 is invalid because it shows a truthfulness grader inside a scorer panel, which is downstream evaluation rather than target-model input. Figure 1 depicts training-data assembly and model adjustment, not the evaluation-time prompt-only route in Table 1. Figure 6 shows multimodal instruction examples with image-plus-text prompts to an LMM, which does not cleanly match the listed text-only gene function description prompt route for LLaMA2-7B (LoRA). The remaining figures are embeddings, performance plots, enrichment charts, or decorative icons, none of which provide a defensible crop of the actual model input for the stated route.",
