eavy-tailed nature of our similarity distribution. Log-log regression analysis shows strong linearity ( R 2 = 0 . 862 ) with a power-law exponent of α = 0.67, while rank-frequency analysis demonstrates excellent fit quality ( R 2 = 0 . 930 ). These results validate that our PageRank-based similarities exhibit the expected heavy-tailed characteristics of hierarchical biological networks, ensuring effective discrimination between closely related and distant cell types.\nTable 6: Summary statistics for Cell Ontology PageRank similarity scores across all cell type pairs.\n\nStatistic, 1 = Value. Mean, 1 = 0.049. Median, 1 = 0.016. Standard Deviation, 1 = 0.087. 95th Percentile, 1 = 0.215. 99th Percentile, 1 = 0.438\nFigure 9: Distribution of similarity scores across all cell type pairs, with a logarithmic frequency scale.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single-panel histogram titled “Distribution of Similarity Values.” The x-axis is labeled “Similarity Score” and ranges from about 0.0 to 1.0. The y-axis is labeled “Frequency (log scale),” with logarithmic tick marks from roughly 10² to above 10⁶.\n\nThe plot shows many blue vertical bins with dark outlines. Frequencies are highest near similarity score 0.0 and decline steadily as similarity increases toward about 0.9. There is a distinct spike at the far right near similarity score 1.0.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, model architecture diagrams, or transformation workflows are visible. The figure appears to summarize a distribution of pairwise or computed similarity scores, with most values low and a smaller concentration of near-identical values at 1.0.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/docling/chunks.json:3958:    "text": "C CELL TYPE SIMILARITY DISTRIBUTION\nFigure 10: Cumulative distribution of similarity scores.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel line plot titled “Cumulative Distribution of Similarity Values.”\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis label: “Similarity Score”\n- Y-axis label: “Cumulative Probability”\n- One blue cumulative distribution curve\n- Gridlines and numeric tick marks\n- The curve rises steeply near similarity score 0, then gradually approaches cumulative probability 1.0 as similarity score approaches 1.0\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible.\n\nTransformations or model interfaces:\n- None shown directly. The figure appears to summarize a distribution of similarity scores using a cumulative distribution function.\n\nFindings shown:\n- Most similarity values are concentrated near low scores.\n- The cumulative probability reaches roughly 0.8 by a similarity score near 0.1 and approaches nearly 1.0 by around 0.5 to 1.0.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001687_a48c004d6e93/full_2026_07_06_rec_001687_a48c004d6e93_docling_json_20260812_193748/docling/document.json:1544:      "orig": "Figure 1: Overview of the CaLMFlow framework. CaLMFlow takes as input textual conditions and flows and generates the next time point for the flows. The textual condition is tokenized and embedded using the LLM tokenizer and embedding layer while the conditional flows are transformed into spatial-temporal tokens using a learned projection. If multiple conditional flows are input simultaneously, the tokens are ordered by flow, space, and then time. The LLM applies causal language modeling and generates the next time point for each flow.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001687_a48c004d6e93/full_2026_07_06_rec_001687_a48c004d6e93_docling_json_20260812_193748/docling/document.json:1545:      "text": "Figure 1: Overview of the CaLMFlow framework. CaLMFlow takes as input textual conditions and flows and generates the next time point for the flows. The textual condition is tokenized and embedded using the LLM tokenizer and embedding layer while the conditional flows are transformed into spatial-temporal tokens using a learned projection. If multiple conditional flows are input simultaneously, the tokens are ordered by flow, space, and then time. The LLM applies causal language modeling and generates the next time point for each flow."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001687_a48c004d6e93/full_2026_07_06_rec_001687_a48c004d6e93_docling_json_20260812_193748/docling/document.json:2719:      "orig": "In this section, we introduce our spatiotemporal and multi-trajectory tokenization method depicted in Figure 1 to effectively represent data for CLM training.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001687_a48c004d6e93/full_2026_07_06_rec_001687_a48c004d6e93_docling_json_20260812_193748/docling/document.json:2720:      "text": "In this section, we introduce our spatiotemporal and multi-trajectory tokenization method depicted in Figure 1 to effectively represent data for CLM training."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_04/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3/full_2026_07_06_rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3_docling_json_20260812_135828/docling/document.json:5050:      "orig": "Dataset and Method Overview. The curation of high-quality datasets is increasingly critical for ad88 vancing PFMs and VLMs, particularly in the era of multimodal reasoning and summarization. At the 89 same time, careful attention must be paid to preventing data leakage to ensure unbiased evaluation 90 of model performance. To this end, and leveraging prior data collection strategies, we distilled a 91 subset of data from PathGen-1.6M (23), which is a large-scale resource comprising nearly 10,000 92 WSIs and 1.6 million ROIs derived from TCGA data (24), for the usage in the finetuning stage with 93 reinforcement learning. Reasoning data were constructed using COT templates generated based 94 on the advanced reasoning model o4-mini (25), with subsequent quality validation performed by 95 pathologists at Yale School of Medicine. Importantly, this dataset does not overlap with the bench96 mark testing set used for Pathology VQA evaluation (26), namely PathMMU (27), which contains 97 ROIs paired with questions across five diagnostic categories and represents one of the most advanced 98 evaluation sources. In addition, another subset distilled from PathGen-1.6M was curated as the test99 ing dataset for the ROI summarization task. To assess performance in cross-modality generation, we 100 leveraged HEST-1K (28) and STImage1K4M (29), two multi-omic histopathology collections to as101 sess the prediction of transcriptomic profiles as molecular signatures from ROIs. These two datasets 102 are used to construct training, validation, and test sets. The overall data preprocessing workflow and 103 sample sizes are summarized in Extended Data Figure 1. 104",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_04/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3/full_2026_07_06_rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3_docling_json_20260812_135828/docling/document.json:5051:      "text": "Dataset and Method Overview. The curation of high-quality datasets is increasingly critical for ad88 vancing PFMs and VLMs, particularly in the era of multimodal reasoning and summarization. At the 89 same time, careful attention must be paid to preventing data leakage to ensure unbiased evaluation 90 of model performance. To this end, and leveraging prior data collection strategies, we distilled a 91 subset of data from PathGen-1.6M (23), which is a large-scale resource comprising nearly 10,000 92 WSIs and 1.6 million ROIs derived from TCGA data (24), for the usage in the finetuning stage with 93 reinforcement learning. Reasoning data were constructed using COT templates generated based 94 on the advanced reasoning model o4-mini (25), with subsequent quality validation performed by 95 pathologists at Yale School of Medicine. Importantly, this dataset does not overlap with the bench96 mark testing set used for Pathology VQA evaluation (26), namely PathMMU (27), which contains 97 ROIs paired with questions across five diagnostic categories and represents one of the most advanced 98 evaluation sources. In addition, another subset distilled from PathGen-1.6M was curated as the test99 ing dataset for the ROI summarization task. To assess performance in cross-modality generation, we 100 leveraged HEST-1K (28) and STImage1K4M (29), two multi-omic histopathology collections to as101 sess the prediction of transcriptomic profiles as molecular signatures from ROIs. These two datasets 102 are used to construct training, validation, and test sets. The overall data preprocessing workflow and 103 sample sizes are summarized in Extended Data Figure 1. 104"
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_04/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3/full_2026_07_06_rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3_docling_json_20260812_135828/docling/document.json:5482:      "orig": "The overall process of dataset curation and model training is summarized in Figures 1 (a)-(d). TeamPath emerges as a robust multimodal AI assistant for both disease diagnosis and modality generation. To refine its reasoning capabilities, we employ Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) (30) to finetune the base model (the default setting is Patho-R1-7B), thereby enhancing its ability to perform reasoning over pathology images. With this capacity for structured reasoning, TeamPath demonstrates strong performance in addressing Pathology VQA tasks, as shown in our comprehensive benchmarking analysis. Importantly, the model also maintains high performance on tasks where reasoning is less critical, such as image summarization (known as caption generation) and cross-modality generation. This adaptability enables TeamPath to support task-specific optimization through either reinforcement learning or supervised finetuning. In collaboration with expert pathologists, we further demonstrate that TeamPath can function as a clinical copilot, assisting in applications such as correcting inaccurate conclusions and identifying flawed reasoning paths. Taken together, TeamPath advances both biomedical research and clinical practice in histopathology analysis. Finally, a comparative summary of task- and metric-specific rankings, shown in Figure 1 (e), demonstrates the superior performance of TeamPath across multiple dimensions.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_04/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3/full_2026_07_06_rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3_docling_json_20260812_135828/docling/document.json:5483:      "text": "The overall process of dataset curation and model training is summarized in Figures 1 (a)-(d). TeamPath emerges as a robust multimodal AI assistant for both disease diagnosis and modality generation. To refine its reasoning capabilities, we employ Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) (30) to finetune the base model (the default setting is Patho-R1-7B), thereby enhancing its ability to perform reasoning over pathology images. With this capacity for structured reasoning, TeamPath demonstrates strong performance in addressing Pathology VQA tasks, as shown in our comprehensive benchmarking analysis. Importantly, the model also maintains high performance on tasks where reasoning is less critical, such as image summarization (known as caption generation) and cross-modality generation. This adaptability enables TeamPath to support task-specific optimization through either reinforcement learning or supervised finetuning. In collaboration with expert pathologists, we further demonstrate that TeamPath can function as a clinical copilot, assisting in applications such as correcting inaccurate conclusions and identifying flawed reasoning paths. Taken together, TeamPath advances both biomedical research and clinical practice in histopathology analysis. Finally, a comparative summary of task- and metric-specific rankings, shown in Figure 1 (e), demonstrates the superior performance of TeamPath across multiple dimensions."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_04/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3/full_2026_07_06_rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3_docling_json_20260812_135828/docling/document.json:5725:      "orig": "Figure 1: Landscape of TeamPath (a) Steps of dataset curation. We extract image-text pairs from a processed TCGA dataset (PathGen-1.6M). (b) Word cloud visualization of ROI captions (upper) and questions (bottom). (c) The core visual language model architecture of TeamPath. (d) TeamPath as a system with an LLM-enhanced router (with over 80% accuracy in choosing the correct approach) and the corresponding capacities in various downstream applications. The logo fire means that we need to adjust the parameters of models, and the logo snowflake means that we do not change the parameters. (e) Overall ranking list of different methods across tasks and metrics. A lower rank (larger bubble) means a better method.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_04/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3/full_2026_07_06_rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3_docling_json_20260812_135828/docling/document.json:5726:      "text": "Figure 1: Landscape of TeamPath (a) Steps of dataset curation. We extract image-text pairs from a processed TCGA dataset (PathGen-1.6M). (b) Word cloud visualization of ROI captions (upper) and questions (bottom). (c) The core visual language model architecture of TeamPath. (d) TeamPath as a system with an LLM-enhanced router (with over 80% accuracy in choosing the correct approach) and the corresponding capacities in various downstream applications. The logo fire means that we need to adjust the parameters of models, and the logo snowflake means that we do not change the parameters. (e) Overall ranking list of different methods across tasks and metrics. A lower rank (larger bubble) means a better method."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001218_da83e96a8915/full_2026_07_06_rec_001218_da83e96a8915_docling_json_20260812_191423/docling/document.json:1980:      "orig": "Figure 1: Overview of our pretraining framework, ALTER . (a) ALTER processes each modality using modality-specific encoders, followed by a universal sequence Transformer and task-specific projection heads for downstream prediction. (b) The three-tiered constraints of ALTER, which can enable model to align multimodal inputs without requiring full modality pairing. (c) ALTER can be applied to any downstream task by integrating task-specific projection heads for fine-tuning.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001218_da83e96a8915/full_2026_07_06_rec_001218_da83e96a8915_docling_json_20260812_191423/docling/document.json:1981:      "text": "Figure 1: Overview of our pretraining framework, ALTER . (a) ALTER processes each modality using modality-specific encoders, followed by a universal sequence Transformer and task-specific projection heads for downstream prediction. (b) The three-tiered constraints of ALTER, which can enable model to align multimodal inputs without requiring full modality pairing. (c) ALTER can be applied to any downstream task by integrating task-specific projection heads for fine-tuning."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001687_a48c004d6e93/full_2026_07_06_rec_001687_a48c004d6e93_docling_json_20260812_193748/docling/document.dclg:169:      Figure 1: Overview of the CaLMFlow framework. CaLMFlow takes as input textual conditions and flows and generates the next time point for the flows. The textual condition is tokenized and embedded using the LLM tokenizer and embedding layer while the conditional flows are transformed into spatial-temporal tokens using a learned projection. If multiple conditional flows are input simultaneously, the tokens are ordered by flow, space, and then time. The LLM applies causal language modeling and generates the next time point for each flow.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001687_a48c004d6e93/full_2026_07_06_rec_001687_a48c004d6e93_docling_json_20260812_193748/docling/document.dclg:493:    In this section, we introduce our spatiotemporal and multi-trajectory tokenization method depicted in Figure 1 to effectively represent data for CLM training.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_04/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3/full_2026_07_06_rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3_docling_json_20260812_135828/docling/document.dclg:648:    Dataset and Method Overview. The curation of high-quality datasets is increasingly critical for ad88 vancing PFMs and VLMs, particularly in the era of multimodal reasoning and summarization. At the 89 same time, careful attention must be paid to preventing data leakage to ensure unbiased evaluation 90 of model performance. To this end, and leveraging prior data collection strategies, we distilled a 91 subset of data from PathGen-1.6M (23), which is a large-scale resource comprising nearly 10,000 92 WSIs and 1.6 million ROIs derived from TCGA data (24), for the usage in the finetuning stage with 93 reinforcement learning. Reasoning data were constructed using COT templates generated based 94 on the advanced reasoning model o4-mini (25), with subsequent quality validation performed by 95 pathologists at Yale School of Medicine. Importantly, this dataset does not overlap with the bench96 mark testing set used for Pathology VQA evaluation (26), namely PathMMU (27), which contains 97 ROIs paired with questions across five diagnostic categories and represents one of the most advanced 98 evaluation sources. In addition, another subset distilled from PathGen-1.6M was curated as the test99 ing dataset for the ROI summarization task. To assess performance in cross-modality generation, we 100 leveraged HEST-1K (28) and STImage1K4M (29), two multi-omic histopathology collections to as101 sess the prediction of transcriptomic profiles as molecular signatures from ROIs. These two datasets 102 are used to construct training, validation, and test sets. The overall data preprocessing workflow and 103 sample sizes are summarized in Extended Data Figure 1. 104
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_04/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3/full_2026_07_06_rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3_docling_json_20260812_135828/docling/document.dclg:760:    The overall process of dataset curation and model training is summarized in Figures 1 (a)-(d). TeamPath emerges as a robust multimodal AI assistant for both disease diagnosis and modality generation. To refine its reasoning capabilities, we employ Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) (30) to finetune the base model (the default setting is Patho-R1-7B), thereby enhancing its ability to perform reasoning over pathology images. With this capacity for structured reasoning, TeamPath demonstrates strong performance in addressing Pathology VQA tasks, as shown in our comprehensive benchmarking analysis. Importantly, the model also maintains high performance on tasks where reasoning is less critical, such as image summarization (known as caption generation) and cross-modality generation. This adaptability enables TeamPath to support task-specific optimization through either reinforcement learning or supervised finetuning. In collaboration with expert pathologists, we further demonstrate that TeamPath can function as a clinical copilot, assisting in applications such as correcting inaccurate conclusions and identifying flawed reasoning paths. Taken together, TeamPath advances both biomedical research and clinical practice in histopathology analysis. Finally, a comparative summary of task- and metric-specific rankings, shown in Figure 1 (e), demonstrates the superior performance of TeamPath across multiple dimensions.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_04/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3/full_2026_07_06_rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3_docling_json_20260812_135828/docling/document.dclg:831:      Figure 1: Landscape of TeamPath (a) Steps of dataset curation. We extract image-text pairs from a processed TCGA dataset (PathGen-1.6M). (b) Word cloud visualization of ROI captions (upper) and questions (bottom). (c) The core visual language model architecture of TeamPath. (d) TeamPath as a system with an LLM-enhanced router (with over 80% accuracy in choosing the correct approach) and the corresponding capacities in various downstream applications. The logo fire means that we need to adjust the parameters of models, and the logo snowflake means that we do not change the parameters. (e) Overall ranking list of different methods across tasks and metrics. A lower rank (larger bubble) means a better method.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001687_a48c004d6e93/full_2026_07_06_rec_001687_a48c004d6e93_docling_json_20260812_193748/docling/document.md:39:Figure 1: Overview of the CaLMFlow framework. CaLMFlow takes as input textual conditions and flows and generates the next time point for the flows. The textual condition is tokenized and embedded using the LLM tokenizer and embedding layer while the conditional flows are transformed into spatial-temporal tokens using a learned projection. If multiple conditional flows are input simultaneously, the tokens are ordered by flow, space, and then time. The LLM applies causal language modeling and generates the next time point for each flow.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001687_a48c004d6e93/full_2026_07_06_rec_001687_a48c004d6e93_docling_json_20260812_193748/docling/document.md:141:In this section, we introduce our spatiotemporal and multi-trajectory tokenization method depicted in Figure 1 to effectively represent data for CLM training.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001617_76b0056d59c3/full_2026_07_06_rec_001617_76b0056d59c3_docling_json_20260812_135401/docling/document.md:9:Figure 1: Overview of this work. Cell-o1 achieves state-of-the-art on the CellPuzzles task.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001617_76b0056d59c3/full_2026_07_06_rec_001617_76b0056d59c3_docling_json_20260812_135401/docling/document.md:82:To bridge this gap, we first introduce CellPuzzles , a novel benchmark that formulates cell type annotation as a batch-level reasoning task, closely mimicking expert annotation workflows. As illustrated in Figure 1, unlike traditional methods that label each cell independently, CellPuzzles requires jointly assigning unique labels to all cells in a batch, using both their gene-expression profiles and shared contextual metadata. Extensive evaluations show that state-of-the-art LLMs struggle with this formulation, with the best-performing model (OpenAI's o1 [22]) achieving only 19.0% batch-level accuracy, reflecting the complexity of this task.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001617_76b0056d59c3/full_2026_07_06_rec_001617_76b0056d59c3_docling_json_20260812_135401/docling/document.md:84:To address this challenge, we propose Cell-o1 , a reasoning-enhanced LLM trained in two phases as shown in Figure 1: supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on expert-like reasoning traces distilled from a frontier LLM to guide structured and interpretable decision-making; followed by reinforcement learning (RL) with batch-level rewards to encourage consistent, context-aware label assignments. On CellPuzzles , Cell-o1 outperforms all baseline models in both cell-level and batch-level accuracy. Further analyses of training dynamics, reasoning behavior, and prediction errors offer insights into the model's generalization, interpretability, and reasoning ability. Notably, Cell-o1 exhibits emergent behaviors such as self-reflection [23, 24], where the model revisits and revises earlier predictions, and curriculum reasoning [25, 26], where it prioritizes simpler cases before tackling harder ones-both resembling strategies used by human experts. In summary, our key contributions include:
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001617_76b0056d59c3/full_2026_07_06_rec_001617_76b0056d59c3_docling_json_20260812_135401/docling/document.md:147:Figure 1 illustrates our training strategy. We begin by introducing a unified prompt template (§4.1) to standardize model outputs and maintain consistent formatting across training stages. Next, we perform reasoning distillation via rejection sampling to construct a high-quality dataset of biological interpretations, followed by SFT for cold-start initialization (§4.2). Finally, we apply RL with GRPO, guided by batch-level reward signals, to further refine the model (§4.3).
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_04/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3/full_2026_07_06_rec_003323_ae216fea4cc3_docling_json_20260812_135828/docling/document.md:175:Dataset and Method Overview. The curation of high-quality datasets is increasingly critical for ad88 vancing PFMs and VLMs, particularly in the era of multimodal reasoning and summarization. At the 89 same time, careful attention must be paid to preventing data leakage to ensure unbiased evaluation 90 of model performance. To this end, and leveraging prior data collection strategies, we distilled a 91 subset of data from PathGen-1.6M (23), which is a large-scale resource comprising nearly 10,000 92 WSIs and 1.6 million ROIs derived from TCGA data (24), for the usage in the finetuning stage with 93 reinforcement learning. Reasoning data were constructed using COT templates generated based 94 on the advanced reasoning model o4-mini (25), with subsequent quality validation performed by 95 pathologists at Yale School of Medicine. Importantly, this dataset does not overlap with the bench96 mark testing set used for Pathology VQA evaluation (26), namely PathMMU (27), which contains 97 ROIs paired with questions across five diagnostic categories and represents one of the most advanced 98 evaluation sources. In addition, another subset distilled from PathGen-1.6M was curated as the test99 ing dataset for the ROI summarization task. To assess performance in cross-modality generation, we 100 leveraged HEST-1K (28) and STImage1K4M (29), two multi-omic histopathology collections to as101 sess the prediction of transcriptomic profiles as molecular signatures from ROIs. These two datasets 102 are used to construct training, validation, and test sets. The overall data preprocessing workflow and 103 sample sizes are summarized in Extended Data Figure 1. 104
