./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000060_d8977b6d9a26/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000060_d8977b6d9a26_docling_json_20260812_141033/docling_graph/nodes.csv:5:"InputRouteDiscovery_412c7610e697171a","InputRouteDiscovery","entity","InputRouteDiscovery","Task instruction prompt","CellTosg2Sequence","inference","Cell Sentence Representation and Prompt Format","task instruction string","['rendered as a short output-format instruction']","task instruction text","[Task] block","actual input","actual model input","Task instruction","3.1 Cell Sentence Representation and Prompt Format","Figure 1","","{""document_id"": ""8ec766b9198f725427a09f06c07872a1"", ""match"": ""verbatim"", ""chunks"": [7], ""pages"": [6], ""refs"": [""#/texts/34"", ""#/texts/35"", ""#/texts/36"", ""#/texts/37""], ""spans"": [{""chunk"": 7, ""start"": 10, ""end"": 60}]}","","",""
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_135854/docling/document.dclg:613:      Figure 1 . Sc hematic of OmniNA. ( A ) Nucleotide sequences, sourced from the NCBI NT database, are amalgamated with their corresponding text annotations for model pre-training. The model, featuring a transformer-based decoder, undergoes pre-training through an autoregressive approach. Fine-tuning is e x ecuted across 23 tasks in a QA frame w ork. ( B ) Ov ervie w of the downstream tasks encompassing nucleotide sequence detection, ranging from tax onom y classification to genomic element detection.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/docling/document.json:1846:      "orig": "Figure 1: Overview of the Cell2Text framework. The model takes single-cell RNA-seq profiles as input and processes them through a pretrained Geneformer encoder to generate contextualized gene-level embeddings. These embeddings are projected into the semantic space of the language model via a lightweight adapter module, aligning biological signals with linguistic representations. A pretrained, instruction-tuned LLM decoder then generates structured natural language descriptions that capture cellular identity, tissue of origin, disease associations, and pathway activity.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/docling/document.json:1847:      "text": "Figure 1: Overview of the Cell2Text framework. The model takes single-cell RNA-seq profiles as input and processes them through a pretrained Geneformer encoder to generate contextualized gene-level embeddings. These embeddings are projected into the semantic space of the language model via a lightweight adapter module, aligning biological signals with linguistic representations. A pretrained, instruction-tuned LLM decoder then generates structured natural language descriptions that capture cellular identity, tissue of origin, disease associations, and pathway activity."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/docling/document.json:6335:      "orig": "The cumulative distribution function (CDF) in Figure 10 further illustrates this property, with the sharp rise in the curve at low similarity values confirming that a large fraction of pairwise similarities are small. This heavy-tailed nature of the distribution is crucial, as it demonstrates the metric's ability to effectively discriminate between the few closely related cell types and the many unrelated ones, which is essential for our nuanced evaluation of model predictions.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/docling/document.json:6336:      "text": "The cumulative distribution function (CDF) in Figure 10 further illustrates this property, with the sharp rise in the curve at low similarity values confirming that a large fraction of pairwise similarities are small. This heavy-tailed nature of the distribution is crucial, as it demonstrates the metric's ability to effectively discriminate between the few closely related cell types and the many unrelated ones, which is essential for our nuanced evaluation of model predictions."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/docling/document.json:6470:      "orig": "Figure 10: Cumulative distribution of similarity scores.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/docling/document.json:6471:      "text": "Figure 10: Cumulative distribution of similarity scores."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000050_23824e963093/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000050_23824e963093_docling_json_20260812_141419/debug/trace_data.json:37:            "text_content": "Figure 1: Training dynamics define distinct generalization regimes in biological reasoning models. We compare backbone choice, continued pre-training (CPT), supervised fine-tuning (SFT), and reinforcement learning (RL) across genomics, transcriptomics, and protein tasks, and evaluate each stage on biologically meaningful in-domain (ID) and out-of-domain (OOD) splits.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic overview of a biological foundation model training and evaluation pipeline.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- **Backbones**: lists general-purpose LLM backbones `Qwen3-1.7B`, `Qwen3-4B`, and `Gemma4`; lists biological foundation models for `DNA: Evo 2`, `RNA: TranscriptFormer`, and `Proteins: ESM3`.\n- **Training Stages**: three sequential stages connected by arrows:\n  - `Continued pre-training (CPT)`: domain-adaptive pre-training on biological corpora.\n  - `Supervised fine-tuning (SFT)`: task-specific instruction tuning with curated datasets.\n  - `Reinforcement learning (RL)`: optimize reasoning and final answers with task-aware rewards.\n- **Biological Modalities**: three vertical modality sections:\n  - `DNA`: example token/string `ATCG`; task `Pathway prediction`; source object appears as genomic alteration nodes/network; output described as predicting pathway activation from genomic alterations.\n  - `RNA`: example token/string `AUGC`; task `Drug target identification`; source object includes a `GENE` label pointing to a target symbol; output described as identifying genes as drug targets from transcriptomic profiles.\n  - `Proteins`: example amino-acid token sequence shown as boxed letters; task `Function prediction`; output label `Function`; described as predicting protein function from amino acid sequence.\n- **Evaluation**: compares:\n  - `In-domain (ID)`: same biological pathway, same diseases, same species.\n  - `Out-of-domain (OOD)`: unseen pathways, unseen diseases, unseen species.\n  - `Findings`: SFT improves in-domain reasoning; RL enhances... [truncated, total 5500 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/docling/document.md:52:Figure 1: Overview of the Cell2Text framework. The model takes single-cell RNA-seq profiles as input and processes them through a pretrained Geneformer encoder to generate contextualized gene-level embeddings. These embeddings are projected into the semantic space of the language model via a lightweight adapter module, aligning biological signals with linguistic representations. A pretrained, instruction-tuned LLM decoder then generates structured natural language descriptions that capture cellular identity, tissue of origin, disease associations, and pathway activity.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/docling/document.md:510:The cumulative distribution function (CDF) in Figure 10 further illustrates this property, with the sharp rise in the curve at low similarity values confirming that a large fraction of pairwise similarities are small. This heavy-tailed nature of the distribution is crucial, as it demonstrates the metric's ability to effectively discriminate between the few closely related cell types and the many unrelated ones, which is essential for our nuanced evaluation of model predictions.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/docling/document.md:536:Figure 10: Cumulative distribution of similarity scores.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000060_d8977b6d9a26/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000060_d8977b6d9a26_docling_json_20260812_141033/docling/chunks.json:531:    "text": "2 Related Work\nFigure 1: CellTosg2Sequence overall workflow. Raw scRNA-seq expression profiles are ranked into a Cell Sentence (topK =200 HGNC gene symbols in descending expression order). A heterogeneous biomedical KG assembled from 11 public databases is encoded by a 2-layer R-GCN with RotatE scoring and projected into 5120-dim virtual tokens via the KGProjector MLP. The virtual tokens are prepended to the textual prompt (cell sentence + metadata + task instruction) and fed into the Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct backbone. The same checkpoint handles cell-type annotation (CTA), perturbation prediction (CPA), and gene-perturbation significance (PSA) through a unified text-in / text-out interface.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a workflow diagram titled “CellTosg2Sequence Overall WorkFlow.” It contains multiple labeled panels connected by arrows.\n\nVisible components:\n- “Raw RNAseq Data”: a cartoon RNA/database icon and an expression profile heatmap with rows labeled Cell 1, Cell 2, Cell 3, ..., Cell N.\n- “LLM Agent Quality Control & Filter”: database/server icons and an OpenAI-style logo, indicating LLM-based filtering or quality control.\n- “Cell Sentence Arrangement in Prompt”: a prompt-like box showing gene tokens such as “Gene A: 3, Gene B:0.9, Gene C:5...” and “TOP k Ranking In Descending Order,” followed by ordered genes like “Gene C, Gene A, Gene B...”.\n- “Biological Prior Knowledge in 11 entities from Public Databases”: lists sources including Protein, UniProt, HGNC, STRING v12, Pathway, Reactome, TRUST, Drug, DrugBank, ChEMBL, Gene, HGNC, Disease, DisGeNET + Open Targets, TF, DoRothEA + TRRUST.\n- Protein/embedding panel: mentions “mean-pooling the residue embeddings of the frozen ESM-2 150M to obtain Per-Gene 640-dimensional priors.”\n- Graph model training panel: labeled “Graph training on R-GCN encoder + RotatE decoder,” with “GraphMessage Passing:R-GCN” and “RotatE:decoder + scorer.”\n- Knowledge graph panel: “Rich Knowledge Graph with Neighborhood Attention and Informative prior,” shown as a colored node-link graph.\n- “Knowledge Graph Project into LLM as Virtual Token”: neural-network style diagram with input nodes Vi1–Vi4 and output tokens Ti1–Ti3, with “Feed in Nodes.”\n- “Example of Original Embedding Style”: a prompt box with tasks including CTA and PSA, using gene-sequence-like colored gene tokens.\n- “Virtual Embedding Injection”: arrow from the virtual token projection into the final prompt.\n- “Final Prompt”: large prompt box containing task examples:\n  - CPA: post-perturbation cell type question.\n  - PSA: asks whether perturbing Gene A in K562 cells causes significant changes in Gene B expression.\n  - CTA: asks about presence of IGHM as a marker for B cells.\n  - Answers shown include gene sequences, “Up regulated,” and “B cells.”",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/debug/trace_data.json:42:            "text_content": "available under aCC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.1101/2023.09.11.557287; this version posted September 14, 2023. The copyright holder for this preprint\n\nFigure 1: Overview of the Cell2Sentence framework. Input single-cell data, including metadata, are converted into cell sentences for LLM fine-tuning. Inference, via prompting, generates new cell sentences that can be converted back to gene expression space.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a workflow schematic for single-cell data generation using Cell2Sentence and LLM fine-tuning.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- Left panel: “Input single-cell data”\n  - Shows a 2D embedding/scatter plot of single cells as colored clusters.\n  - Biological source objects are single-cell profiles represented as points.\n- Middle panel: “Cell2Sentence and LLM fine-tuning”\n  - Shows conversion of cells into text-like “cell sentences.”\n  - Example sentences include immune and disease-related cell labels such as “CD4+ T-cell in Human PBMC,” “CD8+ T-cell in Breast Cancer,” “Monocyte in Lupus,” “Hepatocyte in liver tissue,” and “Beta Cell in Type 1 Diabetes.”\n  - Gene symbols appear in sequences, with masked tokens marked as “[MASK].”\n- Right-middle panel: “Generate cell sentences via prompting”\n  - Shows a model interface with:\n    - Model Input: “T-cell in multiple sclerosis”\n    - Model Output: a generated gene/cell sentence containing markers such as CD3E, CD4, IL17A, IFNG, TNF, RORC, TBX21, CCR5, CXCR3, STAT3, FOXP3, GATA3, TGIT, PDCD1, CTLA4, ICOS, CD28, CD25, CD127, IL2, IL7R, IL12RB1, IL23R, CD69, CD44.\n- Far-right panel: “Generated single-cell data”\n  - Shows another 2D embedding/scatter plot with mostly gray cells and a highlighted blue cluster.\n\nTransformations shown:\n- Single-cell data are transformed into textual cell senten... [truncated, total 5559 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/debug/trace_data.json:62:            "text_content": "Table 3: KNN classification results for separation of distinct cell types. \"NL + C2S\" means pretrained on natural language and then trained on cell sentences. \"C2S\" means no pretraining and just trained on cell sentences. This measures the model's ability to generate distinct clusters when conditioned on cell type. This is done both in cell sentence space using Levenshtein distance (Lev.), as well as after converting back into expression vectors (Expr.).\n\n| Model                   | K=5   | K=5   | K=10   | K=10   | K=25   | K=25   | K=50   | K=50   |\n|-------------------------|-------|-------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|\n|                         | Expr. | Lev.  | Expr.  | Lev.   | Expr.  | Lev.   | Expr.  | Lev.   |\n| scVI                    | 45.72 | -     | 47.04  | -      | 45.36  | -      | 39.90  | -      |\n| GPT-2 Small (C2S)       | 28.65 | 70.61 | 30.03  | 52.26  | 31.68  | 40.88  | 32.66  | 36.21  |\n| GPT-2 Small (NL + C2S)  | 60.07 | 76.09 | 60.41  | 66.95  | 60.55  | 60.04  | 59.42  | 56.80  |\n| GPT-2 Medium (C2S)      | 32.28 | 69.57 | 34.00  | 54.50  | 34.57  | 43.93  | 33.78  | 39.71  |\n| GPT-2 Medium (NL + C2S) | 62.95 | 76.67 | 62.98  | 67.77  | 63.09  | 60.95  | 62.05  | 57.51  |\n\nGenerate text from cells: autoregressive cell type prediction. Models pretrained with natural language (NL) can be trained with Cell2Sentence to generate meaningful text from cells. Our results illustrate the efficacy of this approach through autoregressive prediction of cell types. Note this is distinct from traditional classification-we do not train a classifier head or modify the architecture in any way. We simply prompt the model with a cell sentence and ask it to identify its cell type in natural language (see Figure 6).\n\nTable 4 underscores the necessity of NL pretraining for accurate cell type identification. Figure 12 presents the corresponding confusion matrices. A significant performance decline is observed when using models that have ... [truncated, total 3415 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/debug/trace_data.json:122:            "text_content": "Figure 10: Heatmap visualizations comparing differentially expressed genes per cell type between generated and ground truth expression data showing strong correspondence between generated and real cells across a number of cell types. Differentially expressed genes were identified from the top 100 genes in ground truth data.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a two-panel heatmap-style scientific figure comparing cell-type marker/expression patterns.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Top panel titled **“Ground truth”**\n- Bottom panel titled **“Generated”**\n\nLabels and axes:\n- Rows are immune cell types, including gamma-delta T cell, mucosal invariant T cell, T follicular helper cell, CD4+ helper T cell, effector memory CD4+ alpha-beta T cell, naive thymus-derived CD4+ alpha-beta T cell, naive thymus-derived CD8+ alpha-beta T cell, effector memory CD8+ alpha-beta T cell, CD8+ alpha-beta memory T cell, CD8+ alpha-beta memory T cell CD45RO+, regulatory T cell, classical monocyte, macrophage, CD16- CD56-bright NK cell, CD16+ CD56-dim NK cell, memory B cell, and naive B cell.\n- Columns are gene/protein marker names shown as dense vertical labels.\n- A color scale at the right maps values approximately from **-1 to 1**, with dark blue/purple low values and yellow high values.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Immune cell populations, likely single-cell or cell-type-level profiles.\n- Marker features include recognizable immune genes/proteins such as CD markers, HLA-related markers, cytokine/receptor-associated genes, and lineage markers.\n\nTransformation/model interface:\n- The figure compares **ground truth** profiles against **generated** profiles, implying a generative model output or synthetic reconstruction of cell-type marker patterns.\n- Both panels use the same cell-type rows and feature columns, enabling direct visual comparison.\n\nVisible findings:\n- The generated heatmap broadly resembles the ground truth in major block patterns across immune cell classes.\n- Distinct high-e... [truncated, total 2261 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/debug/trace_data.json:127:            "text_content": "Figure 11: Confusion matrices for KNN classification of cell types using 25 nearest neighbors. Models are (A) GPT-2 Small pretrained, (B) GPT-2 Small fine-tuned, (C) GPT-2 Medium pretrained, and (D) GPT-2 Medium fine-tuned. Fine-tuned models show clearer diagonal patterns, indicating better separation of cell types. This demonstrates improved generation fidelity from natural language pretraining and model scaling.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure showing heatmap-style confusion matrices for cell type classification or separation performance.\n\nVisible structure:\n- Three columns:\n  - “Generated cell separation”\n  - “Ground truth cell separation”\n  - “Generated vs ground truth cell type prediction”\n- Four labeled rows/panels on the left: A), B), C), D)\n- Each panel contains three square heatmaps, for 12 heatmaps total.\n- Axes:\n  - Y-axis label: “Ground Truth Cell Type”\n  - X-axis label: “Predicted Cell Type”\n- Each heatmap uses a viridis-like color scale from dark purple to yellow, with colorbars shown on the right side of the third column.\n- Titles above individual heatmaps report “Average cell-type accuracy,” with visible values including approximately:\n  - A: 32.5846, 60.4518, 23.5059\n  - B: 60.6964, 60.4518, 51.4235\n  - C: 30.2801, 60.4518, 22.1765\n  - D: 57.4788, 60.4518, 45.9294\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Cell types are immune-cell categories, including labels such as CD16+ CD56-bright NK cell, CD16+ CD56-dim NK cell, CD4+ helper T cell, CD8+ memory T cell, CD8+ effector T cell, classical monocyte, effector memory CD4+ T cell, effector memory CD8+ T cell, gamma delta T cell, macrophage, memory B cell, mucosal invariant T cell, naive B cell, naive thymus-derived CD4+ T cell, naive thymus-derived CD8+ T cell, and regulatory T cell.\n\nTransformations / model interfaces:\n- The figure compares generated cell separation, ground-truth cell separation, and generated-vs-ground-truth cell type prediction.\n- The heatmaps app... [truncated, total 2460 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/debug/trace_data.json:132:            "text_content": "Figure 12: Confusion matrices for autoregressive cell type prediction on unseen immune tissue test data. Models pretrained on natural language text before Cell2Sentence training (NL + C2S) significantly outperform models trained only on cell sentences (C2S). This demonstrates the benefits of transfer learning from natural language for predicting cell types from gene sequences.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a 2x2 grid of heatmap panels comparing cell-type classification/confusion patterns for GPT-2 models.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Top left: `GPT-2 Small (C2S)`\n- Top right: `GPT-2 Small (NL + C2S)`\n- Bottom left: `GPT-2 Medium (C2S)`\n- Bottom right: `GPT-2 Medium (NL + C2S)`\n\nAxes and labels:\n- The shared y-axis label is `Ground Truth Cell Type`.\n- Both axes list immune or blood cell types, including examples such as classical monocyte, natural killer cell, megakaryocyte, B cell, plasma cell, plasmablast, macrophage, dendritic cell, T-cell subsets, erythroid lineage cell, and others.\n- Heatmap color scale runs approximately from `0` to `100`, with dark purple indicating low values and yellow indicating high values.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Human immune/hematopoietic cell types are the biological categories being compared.\n- Labels include specific lymphoid, myeloid, dendritic, erythroid, and progenitor-like cell populations.\n\nTransformations / model interfaces:\n- Panels compare two model sizes, `GPT-2 Small` and `GPT-2 Medium`.\n- Each model is shown under two input/interface conditions: `C2S` and `NL + C2S`.\n- The figure appears to visualize prediction distribution or confusion matrices between ground-truth cell types and predicted cell-type labels.\n\nFindings visible from the image:\n- The `NL + C2S` panels show a strong bright diagonal, indicating much better alignment between ground-truth and predicted labels.\n- The `C2S`-only panels show sparse vertical bands and off-diagonal activations, suggesting predictions collapse toward a limited subset of l... [truncated, total 2183 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/debug/trace_data.json:137:            "text_content": "Figure 13: UMAP plots comparing cell embeddings from C2S, scVI, and ground truth data. The Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) statistic quantifies the difference in distributions. The lower MMDvalue for C2S indicates that our generated cells better match the real data distribution than the cells generated with scVI.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single UMAP scatter plot comparing generated and real/generated cell populations.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Main axis label: `UMAP1` on the x-axis; y-axis label is not visible.\n- Legend in the upper right:\n  - Blue: `C2S generated`\n  - Red: `Ground Truth`\n  - Green: `scVI generated cells`\n- Text box in the upper left:\n  - `MMD C2S: 0.0188`\n  - `MMD scVI: 0.3253`\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- Points represent cells or cell embeddings, likely single-cell data.\n- Three cell distributions are compared: C2S-generated cells, ground-truth cells, and scVI-generated cells.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Data are visualized after dimensionality reduction with UMAP.\n- The figure compares model-generated cell distributions against ground truth using MMD scores.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Blue C2S-generated points overlap substantially with red ground-truth points across multiple clusters.\n- Green scVI-generated cells form largely separate clusters, especially a large isolated group on the lower left.\n- The reported MMD is much lower for C2S (`0.0188`) than for scVI (`0.3253`), indicating closer agreement between C2S-generated and ground-truth distributions in this visualization.\n\nFigure 14: UMAP visualization colored by cell type, comparing C2S, scVI, and ground truth data. Both C2S and scVI generate distinct cell types, but only C2S cells strongly align with ground truth cells of the matching type. This shows that the C2S framework can generate specific cell types that closely mimic real cells, outperforming scVI.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows a three-panel scientific scatter plot comparing UMAP embeddings.\n\nVisibl... [truncated, total 3472 chars]",
