./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling_graph/graph.html:475:        "supporting_figure_or_table": "Figure 1",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000060_d8977b6d9a26/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000060_d8977b6d9a26_docling_json_20260812_141033/docling/document.dclg:251:      Figure 1: CellTosg2Sequence overall workflow. Raw scRNA-seq expression profiles are ranked into a Cell Sentence (topK =200 HGNC gene symbols in descending expression order). A heterogeneous biomedical KG assembled from 11 public databases is encoded by a 2-layer R-GCN with RotatE scoring and projected into 5120-dim virtual tokens via the KGProjector MLP. The virtual tokens are prepended to the textual prompt (cell sentence + metadata + task instruction) and fed into the Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct backbone. The same checkpoint handles cell-type annotation (CTA), perturbation prediction (CPA), and gene-perturbation significance (PSA) through a unified text-in / text-out interface.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000050_23824e963093/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000050_23824e963093_docling_json_20260812_141419/docling/chunks.json:609:    "text": "1 Introduction\nBiology is becoming a central testbed for scientific reasoning models. Recent systems combine language models with biological foundation models trained on DNA, RNA, proteins, and other molecular data [1-3]. Their predictions require mapping natural-language task descriptions to molecular representations, integrating modality-specific evidence, and carrying intermediate biological state across multiple inference steps. Post-training is widely used to build such models, but its effects remain poorly understood across training stages [4]. Despite strong empirical gains, it remains unclear how different stages of post-training shape reasoning and generalization.\n∗ Equal contribution.\nFigure 1: Training dynamics define distinct generalization regimes in biological reasoning models. We compare backbone choice, continued pre-training (CPT), supervised fine-tuning (SFT), and reinforcement learning (RL) across genomics, transcriptomics, and protein tasks, and evaluate each stage on biologically meaningful in-domain (ID) and out-of-domain (OOD) splits.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic overview of a biological foundation model training and evaluation pipeline.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- **Backbones**: lists general-purpose LLM backbones `Qwen3-1.7B`, `Qwen3-4B`, and `Gemma4`; lists biological foundation models for `DNA: Evo 2`, `RNA: TranscriptFormer`, and `Proteins: ESM3`.\n- **Training Stages**: three sequential stages connected by arrows:\n  - `Continued pre-training (CPT)`: domain-adaptive pre-training on biological corpora.\n  - `Supervised fine-tuning (SFT)`: task-specific instruction tuning with curated datasets.\n  - `Reinforcement learning (RL)`: optimize reasoning and final answers with task-aware rewards.\n- **Biological Modalities**: three vertical modality sections:\n  - `DNA`: example token/string `ATCG`; task `Pathway prediction`; source object appears as genomic alteration nodes/network; output described as predicting pathway activation from genomic alterations.\n  - `RNA`: example token/string `AUGC`; task `Drug target identification`; source object includes a `GENE` label pointing to a target symbol; output described as identifying genes as drug targets from transcriptomic profiles.\n  - `Proteins`: example amino-acid token sequence shown as boxed letters; task `Function prediction`; output label `Function`; described as predicting protein function from amino acid sequence.\n- **Evaluation**: compares:\n  - `In-domain (ID)`: same biological pathway, same diseases, same species.\n  - `Out-of-domain (OOD)`: unseen pathways, unseen diseases, unseen species.\n  - `Findings`: SFT improves in-domain reasoning; RL enhances out-of-domain performance; trade-offs across stages.\n\nModel/interface flow:\n- Backbone models feed into training stages.\n- Training stages feed into modality-specific biological tasks.\n- Biological modality outputs are evaluated under ID and OOD settings.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- DNA nucleotide sequence example.\n- RNA nucleotide sequence example.\n- Protein amino-acid sequence example.\n- Genomic alteration/network representation.\n- Transcriptomic gene/drug-target representation.\n- Protein sequence-to-function representation.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000050_23824e963093/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000050_23824e963093_docling_json_20260812_141419/docling/chunks.json:7585:    "text": "D Additional Experiments\nD.2 DNA LoRA Rank Allocation\nFigure 10: Optimal adaptation requires asymmetric capacity across training stages. Higher LoRA rank benefits SFT, while lower rank is sufficient for RL, indicating that different stages require different adaptation capacity (both for ID and OOD tasks). Shown are results for pathway prediction (DNA) tasks.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows four adjacent heatmap panels comparing percentage values across LoRA rank settings.\n\nVisible panels:\n- `Qwen3-1.7B — ID`\n- `Qwen3-1.7B — OOD`\n- `Qwen3-4B — ID`\n- `Qwen3-4B — OOD`\n\nAxes and labels:\n- X-axis: `RL LoRA rank`, with columns `4`, `16`, `64`\n- Y-axis: `SFT LoRA rank`, with rows `256`, `64`, `16`\n- Each cell contains a percentage value.\n- Color encodes magnitude, using blue heatmaps for Qwen3-1.7B panels and orange/red heatmaps for Qwen3-4B panels.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible.\n\nTransformations or model interfaces:\n- The figure compares model performance across combinations of SFT LoRA rank and RL LoRA rank.\n- It includes two model sizes, `Qwen3-1.7B` and `Qwen3-4B`, evaluated under `ID` and `OOD` conditions.\n\nFindings visible from the figure:\n- `Qwen3-4B` panels generally show higher percentages than `Qwen3-1.7B`.\n- For `Qwen3-1.7B`, lower performance appears in the `OOD` panel, especially at `RL LoRA rank 4` and lower SFT ranks.\n- For `Qwen3-4B`, many values are above 95%, with the highest visible value `99.3%` in the OOD panel at `SFT LoRA rank 64`, `RL LoRA rank 16`.\n- Some cells are missing or blank, especially at `SFT LoRA rank 16`, `RL LoRA rank 4` in the 1.7B panels.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000350_23e4d9368797/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000350_23e4d9368797_docling_json_20260812_141720/docling/chunks.json:2336:    "text": "3 Results\n3.3 Mixture-of-Experts: router-level decomposition\n3.3.2 Layer-level differentiation\nFigure 1 plots the mean pair-wise JS divergence at each layer for baseline and v3. Baseline is nearly flat at JS ≈ 0 . 08-0 . 13 across layers, with a mild peak at layer 5. v3 more than doubles the JS in layers 10-22 (peak +0 . 17 at layer 12), tripling it relative to baseline (baseline L 12 = 0 . 082, v3 L 12 = 0 . 251). v3 also shows a secondary peak at the output layers ( L 28 = 0 . 287), reflecting a 'choose-the-modality-to-generate' function near lm_head .\nFigure 1: Per-layer mean pair-wise JS divergence across 8 task pools. Red: OmniGene-4 v3; blue: Gemma4-Instruct baseline.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel line chart titled “Cross-task expert routing differentiation, by layer.”\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: “Layer index,” ranging roughly from 0 to 29.\n- Y-axis: “Mean pair-wise JS divergence.”\n- Two plotted series:\n  - Blue line with circular markers: “Gemma-4-Instruct baseline.”\n  - Red line with square markers: “OmniGene-v4 v3.”\n- Shaded regions:\n  - Pink shading labeled “v3 more differentiated,” filling where the red series exceeds the blue series.\n  - Light blue shading labeled “baseline more differentiated,” where the blue series exceeds the red series.\n- Legend appears in the upper-left.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible in the figure.\n\nModel interfaces / computational objects:\n- The figure compares expert routing differentiation across neural network layers for two model conditions: Gemma-4-Instruct baseline and OmniGene-v4 v3.\n- The metric shown is mean pair-wise Jensen-Shannon divergence.\n\nVisible finding:\n- OmniGene-v4 v3 generally has higher cross-task expert routing differentiation than the Gemma-4-Instruct baseline across most layers.\n- The baseline briefly exceeds or matches v3 in a few early layers, but v3 dominates from approximately mid to late layers, with especially high divergence near the final layers.\nThe differentiation is not concentrated in shallow embedding-proximal layers: the largest gains are at layer 12-28, i.e. in the semantic-processing depth of the network. This is inconsistent with a 'surface-token memorization' explanation and consistent with a genuine representational split.\nPer-task entropy analysis strengthens this conclusion. Under CPT+SFT training, Protein and DNA entropies decrease (more concentrated expert use: Protein 2 . 76 → 2 . 54 nats, DNA 2 . 70 → 2 . 53), while NL entropy increases (3 . 85 → 4 . 13). Biological tasks converge onto dedicated experts; NL spreads across a wider but distinct set - so the model is broadening its linguistic capacity rather than narrowing it. This is a direct refutation of a catastrophic-forgetting narrative for this training recipe.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling_graph/nodes.csv:10:"InputRouteDiscovery_73f9dad730989384","InputRouteDiscovery","entity","InputRouteDiscovery","GPT-2 Small metadata-conditioned cell generation","GPT-2 Small","inference","generate cells from text prompts","natural language prompt containing cell type, tissue, or disease metadata","['prompt template selection', 'merge prompt with the specified biological metadata']","text prompt","formatted as prompts","actual input","actual model input","Model Input: “T-cell in multiple sclerosis”","","Figure 1","Shown as an example prompting path in the overview figure; not separately benchmarked in the methods section.","{""document_id"": ""a862cf0d62490022353c377595dca9af"", ""match"": ""verbatim"", ""chunks"": [9], ""pages"": [2, 3], ""refs"": [""#/texts/40"", ""#/texts/42"", ""#/texts/43"", ""#/pictures/0""], ""spans"": [{""chunk"": 9, ""start"": 1890, ""end"": 1933}]}","","",""
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling_graph/nodes.csv:11:"InputRouteDiscovery_9fa677c57c5ef8ec","InputRouteDiscovery","entity","InputRouteDiscovery","GPT-2 Medium metadata-conditioned cell generation","GPT-2 Medium","inference","generate cells from text prompts","natural language prompt containing cell type, tissue, or disease metadata","['prompt template selection', 'merge prompt with the specified biological metadata']","text prompt","formatted as prompts","actual input","actual model input","Model Input: “T-cell in multiple sclerosis”","","Figure 1","Shown as an example prompting path in the overview figure; not separately benchmarked in the methods section.","{""document_id"": ""a862cf0d62490022353c377595dca9af"", ""match"": ""verbatim"", ""chunks"": [9], ""pages"": [2, 3], ""refs"": [""#/texts/40"", ""#/texts/42"", ""#/texts/43"", ""#/pictures/0""], ""spans"": [{""chunk"": 9, ""start"": 1890, ""end"": 1933}]}","","",""
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001687_a48c004d6e93/full_2026_07_06_rec_001687_a48c004d6e93_docling_json_20260812_135702/docling/document.dclg:169:      Figure 1: Overview of the CaLMFlow framework. CaLMFlow takes as input textual conditions and flows and generates the next time point for the flows. The textual condition is tokenized and embedded using the LLM tokenizer and embedding layer while the conditional flows are transformed into spatial-temporal tokens using a learned projection. If multiple conditional flows are input simultaneously, the tokens are ordered by flow, space, and then time. The LLM applies causal language modeling and generates the next time point for each flow.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001687_a48c004d6e93/full_2026_07_06_rec_001687_a48c004d6e93_docling_json_20260812_135702/docling/document.dclg:493:    In this section, we introduce our spatiotemporal and multi-trajectory tokenization method depicted in Figure 1 to effectively represent data for CLM training.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling/chunks.json:862:    "text": "1 Introduction\nIn the following manuscript, we first explain the C2S data transformation and model fine-tuning process. We then evaluate fine-tuned LLMs on tasks including cell generation, classification, and expression recovery to demonstrate their biological understanding. Finally, we discuss the implications and future directions for combining natural language and transcriptomics through representation learning.\navailable under aCC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.1101/2023.09.11.557287; this version posted September 14, 2023. The copyright holder for this preprint\nFigure 1: Overview of the Cell2Sentence framework. Input single-cell data, including metadata, are converted into cell sentences for LLM fine-tuning. Inference, via prompting, generates new cell sentences that can be converted back to gene expression space.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a workflow schematic for single-cell data generation using Cell2Sentence and LLM fine-tuning.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- Left panel: “Input single-cell data”\n  - Shows a 2D embedding/scatter plot of single cells as colored clusters.\n  - Biological source objects are single-cell profiles represented as points.\n- Middle panel: “Cell2Sentence and LLM fine-tuning”\n  - Shows conversion of cells into text-like “cell sentences.”\n  - Example sentences include immune and disease-related cell labels such as “CD4+ T-cell in Human PBMC,” “CD8+ T-cell in Breast Cancer,” “Monocyte in Lupus,” “Hepatocyte in liver tissue,” and “Beta Cell in Type 1 Diabetes.”\n  - Gene symbols appear in sequences, with masked tokens marked as “[MASK].”\n- Right-middle panel: “Generate cell sentences via prompting”\n  - Shows a model interface with:\n    - Model Input: “T-cell in multiple sclerosis”\n    - Model Output: a generated gene/cell sentence containing markers such as CD3E, CD4, IL17A, IFNG, TNF, RORC, TBX21, CCR5, CXCR3, STAT3, FOXP3, GATA3, TGIT, PDCD1, CTLA4, ICOS, CD28, CD25, CD127, IL2, IL7R, IL12RB1, IL23R, CD69, CD44.\n- Far-right panel: “Generated single-cell data”\n  - Shows another 2D embedding/scatter plot with mostly gray cells and a highlighted blue cluster.\n\nTransformations shown:\n- Single-cell data are transformed into textual cell sentences.\n- A language model is fine-tuned on these cell sentences.\n- Prompting the model generates new cell sentences.\n- Generated cell sentences are converted back into generated single-cell data.\n\nMain finding/message:\n- The figure presents a pipeline for generating synthetic or inferred single-cell data from natural-language cell-type/disease prompts using Cell2Sentence representations and an LLM.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling/chunks.json:1477:    "text": "2 Results\n2.2 LLMs can meaningfully manipulate cells as text\nTo further corroborate the gene expression patterns of our generated cells, we employ gene expression heatmaps, presented in Figure 10. These heatmaps compare the differentially expressed genes for each cell type in both the generated and ground-truth cells, with the differential genes sourced from the top 100 genes in the ground truth dataset. The heatmaps confirm that the generated data is consistent with the overall structure in the original dataset while also highlighting subtle gene-level discrepancies. This enhances our understanding of how well the generated cells mimic the intricacies of the baseline data. Additionally, the analysis points to promising avenues for using generated cells to identify key marker genes, offering critical insights into cellular function and laying the groundwork for future research.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling/chunks.json:1843:    "text": "2 Results\n2.2 LLMs can meaningfully manipulate cells as text\n GPT-2 Medium (NL + C2S), K=5.Lev. = 76.67. GPT-2 Medium (NL + C2S), K=10.Expr. = 62.98. GPT-2 Medium (NL + C2S), K=10.Lev. = 67.77. GPT-2 Medium (NL + C2S), K=25.Expr. = 63.09. GPT-2 Medium (NL + C2S), K=25.Lev. = 60.95. GPT-2 Medium (NL + C2S), K=50.Expr. = 62.05. GPT-2 Medium (NL + C2S), K=50.Lev. = 57.51\nGenerate text from cells: autoregressive cell type prediction. Models pretrained with natural language (NL) can be trained with Cell2Sentence to generate meaningful text from cells. Our results illustrate the efficacy of this approach through autoregressive prediction of cell types. Note this is distinct from traditional classification-we do not train a classifier head or modify the architecture in any way. We simply prompt the model with a cell sentence and ask it to identify its cell type in natural language (see Figure 6).\nTable 4 underscores the necessity of NL pretraining for accurate cell type identification. Figure 12 presents the corresponding confusion matrices. A significant performance decline is observed when using models that have not undergone NL pretraining, thereby confirming that the models are not merely memorizing the conditioning text. Despite 1) the limited scope of natural language text in our training prompts relative to the pretraining corpus, and 2) permitting the models to train on these natural language prompts, models without NL pretraining failed to acquire meaningful natural language embeddings. Furthermore, a modest performance increment is observed as the scale of pretrained models increases.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling/chunks.json:4433:    "text": "Appendix\nFigure 10: Heatmap visualizations comparing differentially expressed genes per cell type between generated and ground truth expression data showing strong correspondence between generated and real cells across a number of cell types. Differentially expressed genes were identified from the top 100 genes in ground truth data.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a two-panel heatmap-style scientific figure comparing cell-type marker/expression patterns.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Top panel titled **“Ground truth”**\n- Bottom panel titled **“Generated”**\n\nLabels and axes:\n- Rows are immune cell types, including gamma-delta T cell, mucosal invariant T cell, T follicular helper cell, CD4+ helper T cell, effector memory CD4+ alpha-beta T cell, naive thymus-derived CD4+ alpha-beta T cell, naive thymus-derived CD8+ alpha-beta T cell, effector memory CD8+ alpha-beta T cell, CD8+ alpha-beta memory T cell, CD8+ alpha-beta memory T cell CD45RO+, regulatory T cell, classical monocyte, macrophage, CD16- CD56-bright NK cell, CD16+ CD56-dim NK cell, memory B cell, and naive B cell.\n- Columns are gene/protein marker names shown as dense vertical labels.\n- A color scale at the right maps values approximately from **-1 to 1**, with dark blue/purple low values and yellow high values.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Immune cell populations, likely single-cell or cell-type-level profiles.\n- Marker features include recognizable immune genes/proteins such as CD markers, HLA-related markers, cytokine/receptor-associated genes, and lineage markers.\n\nTransformation/model interface:\n- The figure compares **ground truth** profiles against **generated** profiles, implying a generative model output or synthetic reconstruction of cell-type marker patterns.\n- Both panels use the same cell-type rows and feature columns, enabling direct visual comparison.\n\nVisible findings:\n- The generated heatmap broadly resembles the ground truth in major block patterns across immune cell classes.\n- Distinct high-expression blocks are visible for monocyte/macrophage-associated rows, NK-cell-associated rows, and B-cell-associated rows.\n- Some marker intensities and localized patterns differ between generated and ground truth panels, but the overall structure is preserved.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling/chunks.json:4490:    "text": "Appendix\nFigure 11: Confusion matrices for KNN classification of cell types using 25 nearest neighbors. Models are (A) GPT-2 Small pretrained, (B) GPT-2 Small fine-tuned, (C) GPT-2 Medium pretrained, and (D) GPT-2 Medium fine-tuned. Fine-tuned models show clearer diagonal patterns, indicating better separation of cell types. This demonstrates improved generation fidelity from natural language pretraining and model scaling.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure showing heatmap-style confusion matrices for cell type classification or separation performance.\n\nVisible structure:\n- Three columns:\n  - “Generated cell separation”\n  - “Ground truth cell separation”\n  - “Generated vs ground truth cell type prediction”\n- Four labeled rows/panels on the left: A), B), C), D)\n- Each panel contains three square heatmaps, for 12 heatmaps total.\n- Axes:\n  - Y-axis label: “Ground Truth Cell Type”\n  - X-axis label: “Predicted Cell Type”\n- Each heatmap uses a viridis-like color scale from dark purple to yellow, with colorbars shown on the right side of the third column.\n- Titles above individual heatmaps report “Average cell-type accuracy,” with visible values including approximately:\n  - A: 32.5846, 60.4518, 23.5059\n  - B: 60.6964, 60.4518, 51.4235\n  - C: 30.2801, 60.4518, 22.1765\n  - D: 57.4788, 60.4518, 45.9294\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Cell types are immune-cell categories, including labels such as CD16+ CD56-bright NK cell, CD16+ CD56-dim NK cell, CD4+ helper T cell, CD8+ memory T cell, CD8+ effector T cell, classical monocyte, effector memory CD4+ T cell, effector memory CD8+ T cell, gamma delta T cell, macrophage, memory B cell, mucosal invariant T cell, naive B cell, naive thymus-derived CD4+ T cell, naive thymus-derived CD8+ T cell, and regulatory T cell.\n\nTransformations / model interfaces:\n- The figure compares generated cell separation, ground-truth cell separation, and generated-vs-ground-truth cell type prediction.\n- The heatmaps appear to encode classification agreement or confusion between predicted and ground-truth cell types across several experimental/model conditions A-D.\n\nVisible finding:\n- Ground-truth separation is consistent across rows with an average cell-type accuracy of about 60.4518.\n- Generated cell separation and generated-vs-ground-truth prediction vary substantially by row, with B and D showing stronger diagonal structure and higher reported accuracies than A and C.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling/chunks.json:4547:    "text": "Appendix\nFigure 12: Confusion matrices for autoregressive cell type prediction on unseen immune tissue test data. Models pretrained on natural language text before Cell2Sentence training (NL + C2S) significantly outperform models trained only on cell sentences (C2S). This demonstrates the benefits of transfer learning from natural language for predicting cell types from gene sequences.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a 2x2 grid of heatmap panels comparing cell-type classification/confusion patterns for GPT-2 models.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Top left: `GPT-2 Small (C2S)`\n- Top right: `GPT-2 Small (NL + C2S)`\n- Bottom left: `GPT-2 Medium (C2S)`\n- Bottom right: `GPT-2 Medium (NL + C2S)`\n\nAxes and labels:\n- The shared y-axis label is `Ground Truth Cell Type`.\n- Both axes list immune or blood cell types, including examples such as classical monocyte, natural killer cell, megakaryocyte, B cell, plasma cell, plasmablast, macrophage, dendritic cell, T-cell subsets, erythroid lineage cell, and others.\n- Heatmap color scale runs approximately from `0` to `100`, with dark purple indicating low values and yellow indicating high values.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Human immune/hematopoietic cell types are the biological categories being compared.\n- Labels include specific lymphoid, myeloid, dendritic, erythroid, and progenitor-like cell populations.\n\nTransformations / model interfaces:\n- Panels compare two model sizes, `GPT-2 Small` and `GPT-2 Medium`.\n- Each model is shown under two input/interface conditions: `C2S` and `NL + C2S`.\n- The figure appears to visualize prediction distribution or confusion matrices between ground-truth cell types and predicted cell-type labels.\n\nFindings visible from the image:\n- The `NL + C2S` panels show a strong bright diagonal, indicating much better alignment between ground-truth and predicted labels.\n- The `C2S`-only panels show sparse vertical bands and off-diagonal activations, suggesting predictions collapse toward a limited subset of labels rather than matching each ground-truth class.\n- GPT-2 Small and GPT-2 Medium show broadly similar qualitative behavior: adding `NL` to `C2S` visibly improves diagonal structure.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling/chunks.json:4604:    "text": "Appendix\nFigure 13: UMAP plots comparing cell embeddings from C2S, scVI, and ground truth data. The Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) statistic quantifies the difference in distributions. The lower MMDvalue for C2S indicates that our generated cells better match the real data distribution than the cells generated with scVI.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single UMAP scatter plot comparing generated and real/generated cell populations.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Main axis label: `UMAP1` on the x-axis; y-axis label is not visible.\n- Legend in the upper right:\n  - Blue: `C2S generated`\n  - Red: `Ground Truth`\n  - Green: `scVI generated cells`\n- Text box in the upper left:\n  - `MMD C2S: 0.0188`\n  - `MMD scVI: 0.3253`\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- Points represent cells or cell embeddings, likely single-cell data.\n- Three cell distributions are compared: C2S-generated cells, ground-truth cells, and scVI-generated cells.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Data are visualized after dimensionality reduction with UMAP.\n- The figure compares model-generated cell distributions against ground truth using MMD scores.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Blue C2S-generated points overlap substantially with red ground-truth points across multiple clusters.\n- Green scVI-generated cells form largely separate clusters, especially a large isolated group on the lower left.\n- The reported MMD is much lower for C2S (`0.0188`) than for scVI (`0.3253`), indicating closer agreement between C2S-generated and ground-truth distributions in this visualization.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling/chunks.json:4661:    "text": "Appendix\nFigure 14: UMAP visualization colored by cell type, comparing C2S, scVI, and ground truth data. Both C2S and scVI generate distinct cell types, but only C2S cells strongly align with ground truth cells of the matching type. This shows that the C2S framework can generate specific cell types that closely mimic real cells, outperforming scVI.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows a three-panel scientific scatter plot comparing UMAP embeddings.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Left: “Ground truth”\n- Middle: “Cell2Sentence generated”\n- Right: “scVI generated”\n\nAxes and labels:\n- Each panel has x-axis labeled “UMAP1” and y-axis labeled “UMAP2”.\n- Points are colored by immune cell type.\n- A shared legend appears below the panels.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell populations represented as colored points.\n- Legend includes immune cell types such as macrophage, CD4+ helper T cell, memory B cell, naive B cell, gamma-delta T cell, regulatory T cell, classical monocyte, naive thymus-derived CD4+ α-β T cell, naive thymus-derived CD8+ α-β T cell, effector memory CD4+ α-β T cell, CD8+ α-β memory T cell, CD16−/CD56-bright NK cell, CD16+/CD56-dim NK cell, mucosal invariant T cell, effector memory CD8+ α-β T cell, CD8+ α-β memory T cell CD45RO+, and follicular helper T cell.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- UMAP dimensionality reduction is used to visualize cell embeddings.\n- The figure compares real/ground-truth cell distributions against generated outputs from two models: Cell2Sentence and scVI.\n\nVisible findings:\n- The Cell2Sentence generated panel visually resembles the ground truth structure more closely, preserving several separated clusters and broad spatial organization.\n- The scVI generated panel shows a large dense mixed cluster on the left, sparse dispersed points in the upper/right regions, and smaller separated clusters near the lower-right, appearing less similar to the ground truth distribution.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000050_23824e963093/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000050_23824e963093_docling_json_20260812_141419/docling/document.dclg:254:      Figure 1: Training dynamics define distinct generalization regimes in biological reasoning models. We compare backbone choice, continued pre-training (CPT), supervised fine-tuning (SFT), and reinforcement learning (RL) across genomics, transcriptomics, and protein tasks, and evaluate each stage on biologically meaningful in-domain (ID) and out-of-domain (OOD) splits.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000050_23824e963093/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000050_23824e963093_docling_json_20260812_141419/docling/document.dclg:5552:      Figure 10: Optimal adaptation requires asymmetric capacity across training stages. Higher LoRA rank benefits SFT, while lower rank is sufficient for RL, indicating that different stages require different adaptation capacity (both for ID and OOD tasks). Shown are results for pathway prediction (DNA) tasks.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000350_23e4d9368797/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000350_23e4d9368797_docling_json_20260812_141720/docling_graph/provenance.json:2349:      "text": "3 Results\n3.3 Mixture-of-Experts: router-level decomposition\n3.3.2 Layer-level differentiation\nFigure 1 plots the mean pair-wise JS divergence at each layer for baseline and v3. Baseline is nearly flat at JS ≈ 0 . 08-0 . 13 across layers, with a mild peak at layer 5. v3 more than doubles the JS in layers 10-22 (peak +0 . 17 at layer 12), tripling it relative to baseline (baseline L 12 = 0 . 082, v3 L 12 = 0 . 251). v3 also shows a secondary peak at the output layers ( L 28 = 0 . 287), reflecting a 'choose-the-modality-to-generate' function near lm_head .\nFigure 1: Per-layer mean pair-wise JS divergence across 8 task pools. Red: OmniGene-4 v3; blue: Gemma4-Instruct baseline.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel line chart titled “Cross-task expert routing differentiation, by layer.”\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: “Layer index,” ranging roughly from 0 to 29.\n- Y-axis: “Mean pair-wise JS divergence.”\n- Two plotted series:\n  - Blue line with circular markers: “Gemma-4-Instruct baseline.”\n  - Red line with square markers: “OmniGene-v4 v3.”\n- Shaded regions:\n  - Pink shading labeled “v3 more differentiated,” filling where the red series exceeds the blue series.\n  - Light blue shading labeled “baseline more differentiated,” where the blue series exceeds the red series.\n- Legend appears in the upper-left.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible in the figure.\n\nModel interfaces / computational objects:\n- The figure compares expert routing differentiation across neural network layers for two model conditions: Gemma-4-Instruct baseline and OmniGene-v4 v3.\n- The metric shown is mean pair-wise Jensen-Shannon divergence.\n\nVisible finding:\n- OmniGene-v4 v3 generally has higher cross-task expert routing differentiation than the Gemma-4-Instruct baseline across most layers.\n- The baseline briefly exceeds or matches v3 in a few early layers, but v3 dominates from approximately mid to late layers, with especially high divergence near the final layers.\nThe differentiation is not concentrated in shallow embedding-proximal layers: the largest gains are at layer 12-28, i.e. in the semantic-processing depth of the network. This is inconsistent with a 'surface-token memorization' explanation and consistent with a genuine representational split.\nPer-task entropy analysis strengthens this conclusion. Under CPT+SFT training, Protein and DNA entropies decrease (more concentrated expert use: Protein 2 . 76 → 2 . 54 nats, DNA 2 . 70 → 2 . 53), while NL entropy increases (3 . 85 → 4 . 13). Biological tasks converge onto dedicated experts; NL spreads across a wider but distinct set - so the model is broadening its linguistic capacity rather than narrowing it. This is a direct refutation of a catastrophic-forgetting narrative for this training recipe.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000050_23824e963093/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000050_23824e963093_docling_json_20260812_141419/docling/document.md:53:Figure 1: Training dynamics define distinct generalization regimes in biological reasoning models. We compare backbone choice, continued pre-training (CPT), supervised fine-tuning (SFT), and reinforcement learning (RL) across genomics, transcriptomics, and protein tasks, and evaluate each stage on biologically meaningful in-domain (ID) and out-of-domain (OOD) splits.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000050_23824e963093/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000050_23824e963093_docling_json_20260812_141419/docling/document.md:987:Figure 10: Optimal adaptation requires asymmetric capacity across training stages. Higher LoRA rank benefits SFT, while lower rank is sufficient for RL, indicating that different stages require different adaptation capacity (both for ID and OOD tasks). Shown are results for pathway prediction (DNA) tasks.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_135854/docling/document.json:2659:      "orig": "Figure 1 . Sc hematic of OmniNA. ( A ) Nucleotide sequences, sourced from the NCBI NT database, are amalgamated with their corresponding text annotations for model pre-training. The model, featuring a transformer-based decoder, undergoes pre-training through an autoregressive approach. Fine-tuning is e x ecuted across 23 tasks in a QA frame w ork. ( B ) Ov ervie w of the downstream tasks encompassing nucleotide sequence detection, ranging from tax onom y classification to genomic element detection.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003394_190500e27f28/full_2026_07_06_rec_003394_190500e27f28_docling_json_20260812_135854/docling/document.json:2660:      "text": "Figure 1 . Sc hematic of OmniNA. ( A ) Nucleotide sequences, sourced from the NCBI NT database, are amalgamated with their corresponding text annotations for model pre-training. The model, featuring a transformer-based decoder, undergoes pre-training through an autoregressive approach. Fine-tuning is e x ecuted across 23 tasks in a QA frame w ork. ( B ) Ov ervie w of the downstream tasks encompassing nucleotide sequence detection, ranging from tax onom y classification to genomic element detection."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling/document.md:78:Figure 1: Overview of the Cell2Sentence framework. Input single-cell data, including metadata, are converted into cell sentences for LLM fine-tuning. Inference, via prompting, generates new cell sentences that can be converted back to gene expression space.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling/document.md:215:To further corroborate the gene expression patterns of our generated cells, we employ gene expression heatmaps, presented in Figure 10. These heatmaps compare the differentially expressed genes for each cell type in both the generated and ground-truth cells, with the differential genes sourced from the top 100 genes in the ground truth dataset. The heatmaps confirm that the generated data is consistent with the overall structure in the original dataset while also highlighting subtle gene-level discrepancies. This enhances our understanding of how well the generated cells mimic the intricacies of the baseline data. Additionally, the analysis points to promising avenues for using generated cells to identify key marker genes, offering critical insights into cellular function and laying the groundwork for future research.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling/document.md:244:Table 4 underscores the necessity of NL pretraining for accurate cell type identification. Figure 12 presents the corresponding confusion matrices. A significant performance decline is observed when using models that have not undergone NL pretraining, thereby confirming that the models are not merely memorizing the conditioning text. Despite 1) the limited scope of natural language text in our training prompts relative to the pretraining corpus, and 2) permitting the models to train on these natural language prompts, models without NL pretraining failed to acquire meaningful natural language embeddings. Furthermore, a modest performance increment is observed as the scale of pretrained models increases.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling/document.md:596:Figure 10: Heatmap visualizations comparing differentially expressed genes per cell type between generated and ground truth expression data showing strong correspondence between generated and real cells across a number of cell types. Differentially expressed genes were identified from the top 100 genes in ground truth data.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling/document.md:626:Figure 11: Confusion matrices for KNN classification of cell types using 25 nearest neighbors. Models are (A) GPT-2 Small pretrained, (B) GPT-2 Small fine-tuned, (C) GPT-2 Medium pretrained, and (D) GPT-2 Medium fine-tuned. Fine-tuned models show clearer diagonal patterns, indicating better separation of cell types. This demonstrates improved generation fidelity from natural language pretraining and model scaling.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling/document.md:662:Figure 12: Confusion matrices for autoregressive cell type prediction on unseen immune tissue test data. Models pretrained on natural language text before Cell2Sentence training (NL + C2S) significantly outperform models trained only on cell sentences (C2S). This demonstrates the benefits of transfer learning from natural language for predicting cell types from gene sequences.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling/document.md:695:Figure 13: UMAP plots comparing cell embeddings from C2S, scVI, and ground truth data. The Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) statistic quantifies the difference in distributions. The lower MMDvalue for C2S indicates that our generated cells better match the real data distribution than the cells generated with scVI.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_140520/docling/document.md:726:Figure 14: UMAP visualization colored by cell type, comparing C2S, scVI, and ground truth data. Both C2S and scVI generate distinct cell types, but only C2S cells strongly align with ground truth cells of the matching type. This shows that the C2S framework can generate specific cell types that closely mimic real cells, outperforming scVI.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000350_23e4d9368797/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000350_23e4d9368797_docling_json_20260812_141720/docling/document.md:250:Figure 1 plots the mean pair-wise JS divergence at each layer for baseline and v3. Baseline is nearly flat at JS ≈ 0 . 08-0 . 13 across layers, with a mild peak at layer 5. v3 more than doubles the JS in layers 10-22 (peak +0 . 17 at layer 12), tripling it relative to baseline (baseline L 12 = 0 . 082, v3 L 12 = 0 . 251). v3 also shows a secondary peak at the output layers ( L 28 = 0 . 287), reflecting a 'choose-the-modality-to-generate' function near lm\_head .
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000350_23e4d9368797/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000350_23e4d9368797_docling_json_20260812_141720/docling/document.md:252:Figure 1: Per-layer mean pair-wise JS divergence across 8 task pools. Red: OmniGene-4 v3; blue: Gemma4-Instruct baseline.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1_docling_json_20260812_134529/debug/trace_data.json:87:            "text_content": "available under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.04.14.648850; this version posted January 1, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\n\nA PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\n\nFor generative tasks such as conditional cell generation, we evaluated outputs by measuring gene overlap between 498 generated and target cell sentences. This metric captures the proportion of ground truth genes recovered in the generated 499 output, defined as: 500\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere G ref and G gen denote the sets of genes in the reference and generated cell sentences, respectively.\n\n#### 4.5.2 Parameter-Efficient Scaling Analysis\n\nTo further assess scaling laws under constrained computational budgets, we conducted parameter-efficient fine-tuning experiments using Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) [25]. We utilized the Gemma-2 2B and 27B models as base architectures, with each training run conducted on a single NVIDIA H100 GPU. To isolate the impact of trainable parameter count, we varied the LoRA rank parameter ( r ) from 4 to 128 while holding other hyperparameters (learning rate, batch size, training data) constant. For evaluation, trained LoRA adapters were merged with their corresponding frozen base models, and performance was assessed on the same held-out multi-task test set described above.\n\n### 4.6 Post-training methods\n\n#### 4.6.1 Supervised fine-tuning\n\nFollowing the primary training phase on the multi-task C2S-Scale corpus, base C2S checkpoints were adapted for specific downstream applications through supervised instruction fine-tuning (SFT), represented in Figure 1D (Stage 3). We used the same autoregressive next-token prediction objective employed during the main training stages (stage 1 and 2), with input prompts formatted to align with specific tasks. For example, a c... [truncated, total 4860 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1_docling_json_20260812_134529/debug/trace_data.json:122:            "text_content": "available under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.04.14.648850; this version posted January 1, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\n\nA PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\n\nWe trained each C2S model on 4 H100 GPUs with an effective batch size of 128 and a learning rate of 1e-4. The 410M parameter model was trained for approximately 12 epochs, and the 1B parameter model for approximately 8 epochs, at which point the validation loss converged. To limit token usage and sequence length, C2S models utilized pathway information contained in the SciPlex data rather than RDKit embeddings. Example prompts for training can be found in Supplementary Figure 15, SciPlex.\n\nWe benchmarked against ChemCPA, scGPT, and CondOT. We used the ChemCPA model with the autoencoder architecture pretrained on L1000 data, as this configuration yielded the best performance in the original study. To allow for the generation of unseen perturbations and ensure fairness regarding molecule data access, scGPT was adapted to use RDKit [77] embeddings; similar to the cytokine setup, we added cell type and dose embeddings along with a linear projection of the RDKit embeddings. CondOT was similarly adapted to use RDKit [77] embeddings for the same reasons. For evaluation, we generated the same number of cells for each unique combination of cell line, dose, and drug as present in the test set. We computed the same metrics as in the cytokine stimulation task (MMD, Wasserstein distance, and scFID) for each combination and reported the average across all combinations.\n\n### 4.8 Single-Cell Fréchet Inception Distance\n\nThe scFID is an adaptation of the FID [54] tailored for evaluating generative models in single-cell transcriptomics. While the traditional FID employs the Inception v3 model [78] to extract featu... [truncated, total 5294 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1_docling_json_20260812_134529/debug/trace_data.json:172:            "text_content": "A PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\n\nFigure 1: C2S-Scale unifies transcriptomics and natural language for scalable single-cell analysis. A , The C2S-Scale corpus integrates over 50 million single-cell transcriptomes curated from the CELLxGENE and Human Cell Atlas repositories, linking transcriptomic profiles with rich cell, tissue, and donor-level metadata as well as scientific abstracts and papers. B , C2S-Scale expands upon the Cell2Sentence framework by increasing model capacity up to 27 billion parameters, building a multi-task training corpus of over 1 trillion tokens, and expanding task diversity to include multimodal and multicellular inputs. C , Transcriptomic profiles are transformed into 'cell sentences', ordered sequences of gene names ranked by expression level. This enables the direct application of pretrained LLMs without requiring architectural modifications. D , C2S-Scale is initialized from pre-trained LLM checkpoints. An initial training phase on cell sentences is followed by instruction fine-tuning for a variety of downstream tasks. E , This unified training regime enables broad downstream capabilities, ranging from standard cell annotation to complex tasks like perturbation prediction and biological question answering.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific schematic describing a cell/language foundation model pipeline.\n\nPanel A, “DATA SOURCES AND ANNOTATIONS,” shows five input/source categories with icons and scale labels:\n- Single-cell: “800+ datasets”\n- Cell clusters: “170M+ single-cell & bulk samples”\n- Tissue sections: “100M+ annotations”\n- Paper abstracts: “21M+ perturbations”\n- Donor metadata: “1B+ tokens”\n\nPanel B, “SCALING DIMENSIONS,” shows four boxed scaling factors:\n- Model Capacity: line plot with “Scaling law” and network icons\n- Dataset Size: “170M+ cells from over 800 datasets from CellxGene and Human Cell Atlas”\n- Multimodality: transcriptomics, perturbations, biological papers, donor metadata\n- Cont... [truncated, total 3370 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1_docling_json_20260812_134529/debug/trace_data.json:212:            "text_content": "#### Cell type annotation (single cell)\n\nPrompt: Analyze the following list of genes sorted by decreasing expression levels in a Homo sapiens cell and determine its cell type.\n\nCell sentence: RPLP1 ACTB EEF1A1 HSP90AA1 TMSB4X B2M FTH1 KLF6 HSPA1B MALAT1 RPS12 HSPA8 RPL13 MT-CO1 ATF3 MT-CO2 RPL41 TPT1 MT-CO3 RPS19 HLA-B RPL10 RPS4X ...\n\nBased on these genes, the corresponding cell type is:\n\nResponse:\n\nCD4-positive helper T cell.\n\n#### Tissue annotation (multi-cell)\n\nPrompt: Examine the top 684 genes ordered by descending expression level in 3 Homo sapiens cells. Based on this information, determine the tissue of origin for these cells.\n\nCell sentences:\n\nCell 1: MALAT1 MT-CO1 B2M HLA-A KLF6 EEF1A1 HLA-B HLA-C TMSB4X TSC22D3 RPS12 ...\n\nCell 2: B2M MALAT1 HLA-B RPL10 EEF1A1 MT-CO1 RPL41 TMSB4X HLA-A ZFP36 MT-CO2 ...\n\nCell 3: EEF1A1 MALAT1 RPS12 RPLP1 RPL10 RPL13 RPL41 RPS8 TPT1 CD74 RPS27 RPL18A ...\n\nThe tissue which these cells belong to is:\n\nResponse:\n\nspleen.\n\n#### Cell type annotation (multi-cell)\n\nPrompt: Below is a list of 516 genes in decreasing order of expression in 4 Homo sapiens cells. Given this, predict the cell type which each cell belongs to.\n\nCell sentences:\n\nCell 1: MALAT1 MT-CO1 MT-CO2 MT-CO3 MT-ATP6 JUN MT-ND4 FOS ZFP36L2 MT-ND5 ...\n\nCell 2: MALAT1 EEF1A1 RPS12 RPLP1 RPL41 RPL13 TPT1 RPL10 JUNB RPS8 RPL34 RPS14 ...\n\nCell 3: MALAT1 RPLP1 MT-CO2 MT-CO3 RPL10 RPS12 MT-CO1 RPL13 EEF1A1 RPL41 RPL28 ...\n\nCell 4: MALAT1 HSPA5 RPLP1 B2M MT-CO1 PTMA JUND TMSB4X TPT1 MT-CO3 MT-CO2 ...\n\nThe cell types of these cells are:\n\nResponse:\n\nCell 1: lymphocyte.\n\nCell 2: effector memory CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell.\n\nCell 3: naive thymus-derived CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell.\n\nCell 4: CD4-positive helper T cell.\n\nFigure 11: Example input prompts and ground truth responses for C2S-Scale pretraining predictive tasks: cell type(s) annotation and tissue annotation.\n\nA PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1_docling_json_20260812_134529/debug/trace_data.json:227:            "text_content": "A PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\n\nFigure 14: Example input prompts and ground truth responses for C2S-Scale downstream natural language tasks: cluster captioning and question answering.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a two-panel scientific figure showing examples of a language-model style interface for single-cell biology tasks.\n\nTop panel label: “Cluster captioning”\n- Shows a prompt asking to “Describe this single-cell cluster.”\n- Input consists of 5 cells with genes listed in descending order of expression.\n- Visible gene examples include MALAT1, DPP10, RBFOX1, SNHG14, ZNF385D, LRP1B, MEG3, NRXN1, PCDH9, NRG3, DLG2.\n- The response describes a cerebral cortex neuron class with adhesion and signaling programs, mentioning DSCAM, NXPH2, FGFR1, FGF ligands, HTR2C, GRM3/GRM4/GRM8, and GHR.\n- Biological source object: single-cell gene expression profiles from a neuronal cluster.\n- Transformation/interface: raw per-cell ranked gene lists are converted into a natural-language cluster caption.\n\nBottom panel label: “Question answering”\n- Shows a prompt about endothelial cells profiled by single-cell RNA sequencing in diseased tissue.\n- Input lists 10 cells with cell type, tissue, disease status, and ranked gene expression.\n- Visible cell types include fibroblast, endothelial cell, glial cell, pericyte, and myofibroblast cell.\n- Tissues include ileal epithelium, lamina propria of small intestine, small intestine, and ileum.\n- Disease labels include Crohn disease and normal.\n- Visible genes include MALAT1, MT-ATP6, MT-ND3, MT-CO3, MT-CO1, B2M, MT-CO2, MT-CYB, ADAMDEC1, IGFBP7, RGS5, ACTB, MYL9, TPM2, CFD, TMSB4X.\n- The question asks what can be inferred from raw endothelial cell sentences about potential markers of vascular inflammation.\n- The response identifies CD74, IFI27, and HLA-DRA as possible vascular inflammation markers.\n- Biological source object: single-cell RNA-seq-derived cell sentences from intestinal/Crohn disease context.\n- Transformation/interf... [truncated, total 2214 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1_docling_json_20260812_134529/debug/trace_data.json:232:            "text_content": "#### Perturbation effect prediction - PBMC\n\nPrompt: Analyze how IL-6 affects the expression hierarchy of CD4 T cells, presented as an ordered list of genes by decreasing expression in 'Source:', under chronic stimulation.\n\nSource: MALAT1 RPS12 RPS13 RPS6 RPL3 RPS4X B2M FTL RPLP0 RPS5 RPL39 RPS26 ACTB MT-CO3 HLA-A MT-ATP6 HLA-C TRBV9 TMSB10 S100A6 IFITM1 HLA-E PDE3B MT-CYB KLRK1 TXN ...\n\nResponse: MALAT1 RPS12 RPS13 RPS6 B2M RPLP0 RPL39 RPS4X RPL3 FTL MTRNR2L12 ACTB MT-ATP6 RPS5 MT-CYB HLA-C HLA-A MT-CO3 IFITM1 IL32 TMSB10 HLA-E SELL S100A6 ...\n\n#### Perturbation effect prediction - L1000\n\nPrompt: Based on the conditions specified below, analyze the response of the following bulk RNA-seq cell line, represented by gene names ranked by expression in 'Source:', after stimulation with the listed molecule.\n\nPerturbation: triptorelin\n\nPerturbation dose: 0.37 uM\n\nPerturbation time: 24 h\n\nCanonical SMILE: CC(C)C[C@H](NC(=O)[C@@H](Cc1c[nH]c2ccccc12)NC(=O)[C@H] ...\n\nCell line: MCF7\n\nSource: GAPDH HSPB1 MIF RPS6 PCNA XBP1 TIMM9 RNPS1 GNAS RPN1 APP S100A13 STUB1 ...\n\nResponse:\n\nGAPDH GNAS RPS6 RNPS1 S100A13 DFFB XBP1 TIMM9 NCOA3 FKBP4 CSRP1 LSM5 ...\n\n#### Perturbation effect prediction - SciPlex3\n\nPrompt: Determine the single cell's expression changes, listed in 'Untreated:', under the outlined conditions after exposure to the specified molecule.\n\nPerturbation: Enzastaurin (LY317615)\n\nPerturbation dose: 1000.0\n\nSMILES: CN1C=C(C2=CC=CC=C21)C3=C(C(=O)NC3=O)C4=CN(C5=CC=CC=C54)C6CCN(CC6) ...\n\nCell line: A549\n\nPathway: TGF-beta/Smad\n\nPathway level 1: PKC signaling\n\nPathway level 2: PKC activitiy\n\nUntreated: PDE4D AKR1B10 AKR1C2 CA12 GAPDH PDE10A ALDH1A1 GLIS3 CNTN1 PDE4B MYO1E ABCC3 PCDH9 SQSTM1 FN1 PTPRM PDE7B TXNRD1 FAM107B EFNA5 PTK2 GABRB3 ...\n\nResponse: PDE4D GAPDH CYP24A1 ALDH1A1 MAP7 ABCC2 MYO1E ALDH3A1 AKR1C2 CAST TXNRD1 AKR1B10 LINC00511 PRKCA NCOA3 AC099520.1 NRG1 PLD5 SMAD3 STAT3 AKR1C3 ...\n\nFigure 15: Example input prompts and ground truth responses for C2S-Scale downstre... [truncated, total 2119 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1_docling_json_20260812_134529/debug/trace_data.json:242:            "text_content": "A PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\n\nFigure 17: Additional results for spatial tasks. A, Spatial cell generation results. Bars indicate average gene overlap percentage across 100 generated replicates for 100 samples. ** = P &lt; 0.01; two-tailed T-test). B, Niche label prediction results. Dashed line indicates random accuracy. * = P &lt; 0.05, ** = P &lt; 0.01; McNemar's test). Dashed line represents random baseline accuracy (0.184). C, Same niche prediction results. Dashed line indicates random accuracy. * = P &lt; 0.05, ** = P &lt; 0.01; McNemar's test). Dashed line represents random baseline accuracy (0.51). D, Mean and standard deviation of C2S models trained with three different random seeds. Including CPDB and BioGRID interactions consistently improves performance over the base C2S model.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific bar-plot figure with panels labeled **A**, **B**, **C**, and **D**.\n\nPanel **A**: Bar chart titled **“Neighborhood Generation”**. Y-axis is **“Gene overlap %”**. Compares variants labeled approximately **C2S (w/ CPDB)**, **C2S**, **C2S (w/ BioGrid)**, and **C2S (w/ BioGrid & CPDB)**. Bars increase slightly from left to right, with a significance bracket marked `**` over the comparison.\n\nPanel **B**: Bar chart titled **“Niche Label Prediction”**. Y-axis is **“Niche Prediction Accuracy”**. Methods include **scGPT**, **Nicheformer**, **C2S**, **C2S (w/ CPDB)**, **C2S (w/ BioGrid & CPDB)**, **Linear Model**, and **C2S (w/ BioGrid)**. A dashed horizontal baseline is shown near the lower part of the plot. Multiple significance brackets are shown with `*` and `**`. Highest bars appear for C2S variants and the linear model, with **C2S (w/ BioGrid)** highest.\n\nPanel **C**: Bar chart titled **“Same Niche Prediction”**. Y-axis is **“Same Niche Prediction Accuracy”**. Methods include **GPT-4o**, **Linear Model**, **scGPT**, **C2S**, **Nicheformer**, **C2S (w/ CPDB)**, **C2S (w/ BioGrid)**, and **C2S (w/ BioGrid & CPDB)**. A dashed bas... [truncated, total 3004 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1_docling_json_20260812_134529/debug/trace_data.json:247:            "text_content": "A PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\n\nFigure 18: Extended characterization and validation of virtual screening candidates in tumor fragment and cell line models. A, Experimental validation of top hits in an immune-context-positive tumor fragment model. Surface levels of HLA-A,B,C (Mean Fluorescence Intensity; MFI) were measured following treatment with 10 nM of each drug. IFNγ serves as a positive control. Significance is compared to DMSO vehicle. B, Computational characterization of the immune-context-neutral state. (Top Left) Baseline Type I interferon (IFN) response gene signature scores for the Primary Tumor context compared to three cell lines (WAGA, UTSCC45, MCC2). (Top Right, Bottom) Volcano plots displaying predicted drug effects in these cell line contexts. Silmitasertib (red marker) shows no significant predicted efficacy in these interferon-low settings compared to the primary tumor context. C, Validation of the interferon-conditional effect in MDK-knockout WAGA cells. Dose-response assessment of HLA-A,B,C MFI in MDK-knockout WAGA cells treated with silmitasertib in the absence (gray) or presence (blue) of low-dose IFNβ (2 U/ml). D, Generalization of the amplification effect to Type II interferon. Dose-response assessment of HLA-A,B,C MFI in MDK-knockout WAGA cells treated with silmitasertib in the absence (gray) or presence (blue) of low-dose IFNγ (2 U/ml). Error bars represent mean ± s.d. Significance calculated via two-way t-test with Benjamini-Hochberg correction; ∗ P &lt; 0 . 05 , ∗ ∗ P &lt; 0 . 01 , ∗ ∗ ∗ P &lt; 0 . 001 , ∗ ∗ ∗ ∗ P &lt; 0 . 0001 .\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical figure with panels A-D.\n\nPanel A: Bar plot titled “Immune Context-Positive Tumor Fragment Model.” Y-axis is “HLA-A/B/C MFI.” Conditions on x-axis: DMSO, Silmitasertib, Afatinib, GSK-126, Crizotinib, Trametinib, IFN-γ. Bars show increased HLA-A/B/C MFI for several treatments, with highest values for IFN-γ and strong increases for Crizotinib/Trametinib. In... [truncated, total 3725 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1_docling_json_20260812_134529/debug/trace_data.json:252:            "text_content": "A PREPRINT - DECEMBER 31, 2025\n\nFigure 19: A, Diagram of the scFID metric, computed in foundation model latent space, analogous to FID in computer vision. B, UMAP showing the 10 most frequent cell types in the Dominguez immune dataset [20]. C, The scFID is significantly lower within cells of the same cell type than between different cell types (two sample t-test; 10 samples within cell types, 45 samples between cell types; P = 2 . 23 × 10 -4 ). D, Confusion matrix showing the scFID between every pair of cell types. E-G Same as B-D, but for Leiden clusters instead of cell (two sample t-test; 18 samples within clusters, 153 samples between clusters; P = 2 . 60 × 10 -9 .)\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled A-G about evaluating single-cell foundation model embeddings with scFID.\n\nPanel A: Schematic workflow. Microscopy-like biological image patches or tissue/cell regions are input to a “Foundation Model.” The model produces embedding distributions across embedding dimensions for two groups shown in blue and red. Each distribution is summarized by mean μ and covariance Σ, then compared using Wasserstein distance to compute scFID.\n\nPanel B: UMAP plot titled “Cell Type Clustering - UMAP.” Points are colored by annotated immune cell types. Axes are UMAP 1 and UMAP 2. The legend includes cell-type categories such as naive thymus-derived CD4-positive cells, memory B cells, CD8-positive alpha-beta memory cells, classical monocytes, CD16-positive/CD56-dim natural killer cells, effector memory CD4-positive cells, alveolar macrophages, CD4-positive helper T cells, T follicular helper cells, and effector memory CD8-positive cells.\n\nPanel C: Boxplot titled “Cell Type - scFID Comparison.” It compares scFID for Inter versus Within cell-type groups. Inter values are higher than Within values, with a significance marker shown above the comparison.\n\nPanel D: Heatmap titled “Cell Type - scFID Matrix.” Rows and columns are the same immune cell-type l... [truncated, total 2863 chars]",
